                                [38;5;153m↓ POST API_ENDPOINT { username, password }[39m
                          [38;5;153mserver verifies, records the login event, returns { userId, username }[39m
                                [38;5;153m↓[39m
                          [38;5;153mapp.js → session.setSession({ userId, username })[39m
                                [38;5;153m↓[39m
                          [38;5;153msessionStorage["app.session"] = {"userId":…,"username":…}[39m

  [38;5;153mlogin[39m changes from synchronous stub to [38;5;153masync[39m, and the submit handler awaits it. [1mThe [38;5;153muserId[39m is a return value, not a [0m
  [1mparameter[0m — which is the one place this departs from the request as originally worded, for the reason I raised
  earlier: the browser cannot know the account ID before the server has authenticated the credentials.

  [1mServer contract[0m (specified in the spec, not implemented here — no backend exists):

  - [38;5;153mPOST /login[39m, body [38;5;153m{ username, password }[39m
  - [38;5;153m200[39m → [38;5;153m{ userId, username }[39m
  - [38;5;153m401[39m → [38;5;153m{ error }[39m on bad credentials
  - The server is what persists the login record (who, when, and whatever request metadata you want). That's the durable
    "track who logged in" half; the client half is only about making the ID available to other forms.

  [1mConstraint carried into the spec:[0m [38;5;153muserId[39m in client storage is an identifier, never authorization. Any future endpoint
  must derive identity from a server-side session, not from a [38;5;153muserId[39m the client sends. This is what keeps approach C
  reachable without a rewrite.
